> The point to me is that the current version (MacNetHack 3.4.1)
> seems to have a biased RNG. In one game, I'll find 4 or 5 altars
> in the first six levels and no shops; in another, I'll find
> 2 or 3 shops and no altars. I recently found 3 bags of holding
> in the mines before I reached minetown (identified only after
> I died, unfortunately). That seems to me to indicate a not
> very RNG. Has anybody done any testing on it?

Changing the seed won't change the algorithm. Unless you define RANDOM,
Nethack uses one of the linear congruential RNG functions provided by
your C library. In your case *rand48() are used and somewhat incorrectly
so (relying on lower-bits, see Knuth's second TAOCP book somewhere for
more info).

So although the RNG has an intrinsic meanness to it and that shouldn't
be interpreted as malfunction, there's indeed a bug with it currently.

If you wish better random numbers AFAIK your options are:

- enable nethack's built-in sophisticated non-LCA random library (define
RANDOM). I haven't tested it personally, but I think it should produce
good results.

- download my patch at http://home.cs.tum.edu/traykovs/nethack/. This
improves the usage of the random numbers received from the *rand48
functions. It has undergone successfully some basic tests, but it
doesn't go at lengths to satisfy more advanced ones (it doesn't have
loops and other drastic complications). It's very fine for playing though.

